{"attack": true, "case": "The target's claim defends a preference for 'better-corroborated theories,' but its mechanism justifies a different preference — for theories with 'higher empirical content.' Corroboration (a theory's having survived past tests) and empirical content (a theory's logical falsifiability) are distinct in Popper's framework: a falsified theory can have high empirical content, and a well-corroborated theory can have modest content. The mechanism says the point of the preference is to 'maximize exposure to falsification' and 'learn faster,' but maximizing exposure to falsification directly favors the most falsifiable (highest content) theory regardless of its corroboration history. Corroboration — past survival of tests — is neither necessary nor sufficient for maximizing future exposure to falsification: a bold new conjecture with no test history may have greater falsifiability than a well-corroborated rival. By silently substituting 'empirical content/corroborability' (a structural, synchronic property) for 'corroboration' (a historical, diachronic property involving past test outcomes), the mechanism defends a different rule than the one stated in the claim. The stated preference (for corroboration) requires relying on past test performance, which is exactly where the inductive challenge bites; the mechanism's actual justification (for empirical content) sidesteps that challenge but no longer defends the claim as written. Thus the target fails to defend the preference for better-corroborated theories specifically, because its own justification applies to a logically distinct preference.", "premise": "In Popper's framework, corroboration (past survival of severe tests) and empirical content (logical falsifiability) are distinct properties: the former is diachronic and historical, the latter is synchronic and structural. A preference that 'maximizes exposure to falsification' is justified by empirical content, not by corroboration history.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "cd3ca5fef732e6786486dde4fbdb985dad2460e082a09fbe759a376263881266", "quote": "The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a non-inductive, methodological rule of 'critical rationality' that maximizes exposure to falsification, not probability of truth."}, {"block": "cd3ca5fef732e6786486dde4fbdb985dad2460e082a09fbe759a376263881266", "quote": "Choosing the theory with higher empirical content (and thus higher corroborability) is the only way to learn faster, even if it doesn't make the theory more likely to be true. It is a pragmatic imperative for growth of knowledge, not an inductive inference about the future."}], "counterexample": ["Consider two theories: Theory A has high empirical content but has never been tested (zero corroboration). Theory B has moderate empirical content and has survived many tests (high corroboration). If the methodological rule is to 'maximize exposure to falsification,' one should prefer A (more falsifiable, more to expose). If the rule is to prefer 'better-corroborated theories,' one should prefer B. These are opposite recommendations, showing that corroboration and empirical content/maximum-falsifiability are not equivalent and the target conflates them."], "successor_question": "Can the target articulate a non-inductive justification for preferring corroboration (past test survival) specifically — as opposed to empirical content or falsifiability — that does not collapse into relying on past performance as a guide?"}